  {
   "id": "farming",
   "text": "Then, around ten thousand BC, as the last ice age ended, something changed forever. People began to plant seeds, tame animals and build permanent villages. Farming meant more food and more children, and for the first time, a reason to stay in one place.",
   "scene": {
    "type": "era",
    "year": "10,000 BC",
    "title": "The Farming Revolution",
    "sub": "From hunters to farmers",
    "icons": [
     "fluent-emoji-flat:sheaf-of-rice",
     "fluent-emoji-flat:ear-of-corn",
     "fluent-emoji-flat:hut"
    ],
    "cue": "ten"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "farmers",
   "text": "It began in the Fertile Crescent. At Göbekli Tepe, people raised huge carved stone pillars about eleven and a half thousand years ago. Jericho became one of the oldest towns on Earth. And Çatalhöyük was home to thousands of people, in mud-brick houses with no streets, entered through the roof. Farming also began on its own in other places, from East Asia to Central America.",
   "scene": {
