  {
   "id": "writing",
   "text": "By about thirty-two hundred BC, some villages had grown into cities. Cities needed rulers, soldiers, priests and, above all, a way to keep records. So in Mesopotamia, scribes began pressing marks into wet clay. Writing was born, and with it, recorded history.",
   "scene": {
    "type": "era",
    "year": "3200 BC",
    "title": "Cities, Kings & Writing",
    "sub": "The first civilizations",
    "icons": [
     "fluent-emoji-flat:writing-hand",
     "fluent-emoji-flat:crown",
     "fluent-emoji-flat:classical-building"
    ],
    "cue": "thirty"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "rivers",
   "text": "Four great river civilizations took shape. Uruk, between the Tigris and the Euphrates, was one of the first true cities. Along the Nile, Egypt was united around thirty-one hundred BC, and its kings later built the pyramids of Giza. In the Indus Valley, Mohenjo-daro had brick streets and covered drains. And in China, Shang kings carved writing onto oracle bones at Anyang.",
